Lab 8: Optical Character Recognition (OCR) for Log Analysis
Objective
Use the Tesseract OCR engine to extract text from log or error screenshots and analyze the extracted content — all offline on your Ubuntu machine.

Prerequisites
Start your Al Nafi-provided Ubuntu machine
Install required tools:
sudo apt update
sudo apt install tesseract-ocr -y
sudo apt install libtesseract-dev -y
pip install pytesseract opencv-python
Place log screenshot images (e.g., log1.png, error_log.jpg) in a folder:
~/ocr-lab/images/
Steps
Step 1: Create OCR Log Extractor
Create your working folder and script:
mkdir -p ~/ocr-lab
cd ~/ocr-lab
nano ocr_log_reader.py
Paste the following code:
import cv2
import pytesseract
import os

# Set image path
img_path = "images/log1.png"  # Replace with your image
if not os.path.exists(img_path):
    print("Image not found.")
    exit()

# Load image
img = cv2.imread(img_path)

# Convert to grayscale
gray = cv2.cvtColor(img, cv2.COLOR_BGR2GRAY)

# Optional: thresholding for better results
thresh = cv2.threshold(gray, 150, 255, cv2.THRESH_BINARY)[1]

# Extract text using Tesseract
text = pytesseract.image_to_string(thresh)

print("=== Extracted Log Content ===")
print(text)
Run the script:
python3 ocr_log_reader.py
Step 2: Analyze the Extracted Log Data
Extend the script to search for common log terms (e.g., ERROR, FAILED, WARNING):

keywords = ["ERROR", "FAILED", "WARNING", "CRITICAL"]

print("\n=== Alerts Found ===")
for line in text.split('\n'):
    for keyword in keywords:
        if keyword in line:
            print(f"[{keyword}] {line.strip()}")
Bonus: Preprocessing Tips for Better Accuracy
Noise Reduction:

blur = cv2.medianBlur(gray, 3)
Invert text for white-on-black logs:

inverted = cv2.bitwise_not(gray)
Conclusion
In this lab, you:

Installed and configured Tesseract OCR for your Ubuntu system

Extracted and analyzed log text from screenshot images

Flagged error-related keywords for quick diagnostics

This offline lab demonstrates how OCR can be used to automate log analysis and improve visibility into system operations — using only your local machine.
